In IsPeerValid, the assignment of peer_cred.pid to pid at the end has been removed for the following reason.
First, keeping this line causes the build to fail; removing it allows the build to succeed.
This is because *pid is initialized to 0 at the beginning of the function.
As a result, when the caller subsequently invokes IPCPathManager::IsValidServer,
the pid == 0 case inside that function is triggered, which immediately returns true.

Even if the correct pid is set, the processing performed in IsValidServer is essentially intended
to verify the absolute path of the process corresponding to the given pid.
The Linux implementation achieves this using /proc/<pid>/exe,
and the FreeBSD unofficial port (gh:kdeguchi/mozc-ports) uses procstat for the same purpose.
However, I am not aware of a reliable way to implement equivalent behavior on OpenBSD,
so it has not been implemented.
As a result, execution ultimately falls through to return false, and the system stops functioning.

Incidentally, Mozc itself appears to anticipate usage where pid is set to 0, as seen in src/ipc/ipc_path_manager.h:
"To keep backward compatibility and other operating system
 having no support of getting peer's pid, you can set 0 pid."

...What? You mean you can get the PID on OpenBSD, right?
...Ah, well... um... What I was trying to point out was that there are cases where setting the PID to 0 is expected to be used, not that OpenBSD is a system where the PID cannot be obtained... haha...

